## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cell, stability, surface coating, ultrahydrophobic, self-recovery
## ABSTRACT
In spite of a continuous increase in their power conversion efficiency (PCE) and an economically viable fabrication process, organic–inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) pose a significant problem when used in practical applications: They show fast degradation of the PCE when exposed to very humid environments. In this study, the stability of PSCs under very humid conditions is greatly enhanced by coating the surface of the PSC devices with a multi-layer film consisting of ultrahydrophobic and relatively hydrophilic layers. A hydrophobic composite of poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA), polyurethane (PU), and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles successfully retards the water molecules from very humid surroundings. Also, the hydrophilic layer with moderately PMMA captures the residual moisture within the perovskite layer; subsequently, the perovskite layer recovers. This dual function of the coating film keeps the PCE of PSCs at \(17.3\%\) for \(180\;\mathrm{min}\) when exposed to over \(95\%\) humidity.
## 1 Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are emerging candidates for alternative energy devices. This is predominantly due to an increase in their power conversion efficiency (PCE) to over \(22\%\) and their economically viable fabrication
process [1–5]. However, barriers restricting commercialization include the instability of perovskites in humid environments [6, 7], thermal [8, 9] and light degradation [10] under solar radiation, reversible hysteresis of current density–voltage \((J{-}V)\) curves [11–15], and toxicity of Pb [16–18]. Of particular importance is the gradual dissociation of the perovskite
phase on water penetration. When in contact with water, lead halide perovskite materials \((\mathrm{MAPbX}_{3},\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{I},\mathrm{Br},\mathrm{Cl})\) decompose into \(\mathrm{PbX}_{2}\) and other byproducts. To date, several groups have examined passivation materials that can protect the perovskite materials from water molecule attachment and prevent deterioration of their photovoltaic performance [19–31]. Replacement of the hole transport layer (HTL) with a carbon electrode allows PSCs to operate for 40 days in ambient air without performance degradation [26]. Inorganic hole transport materials (HTMs) such as Cu-doped \(\mathrm{NiO}_{x}\) and CuI have also successfully preserved high conversion efficiencies after a few days in ambient conditions [27].
In comparison to studies on the stability of PSCs in ambient air with moderate humidity, so far, there have been few reports on PSC devices retaining performance under extremely humid conditions. An \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) coating between the perovskite layer and the metal electrode was found to resist moisture permeation while minimizing PCE deterioration; the PCE deteriorated by only \(30\%\) after \(5\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(60\%\) relative humidity [28]. Single-walled carbon nanotubes added to the organic polymer matrix of the HTM layer afforded PSC operation in water for \(15\,\mathrm{s}\) [19]. Deposition of a hydrophobic polytetrafluoroethylene layer onto the top side of PSCs also protected the halide perovskite from moisture for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\) in water [20]. While these studies have demonstrated some water resistance improvement through use of passivation layers, this effect is limited for exposure to conditions of over \(95\%\) humidity.
This work reports the enhanced moisture stability of PSCs after double-coating of relatively hydrophilic and ultrahydrophobic layers on the surface of PSC devices. The first coating layer is composed of poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA) and polyurethane (PU) (denoted as PMMA-PU). The second coating layer is an ultrahydrophobic PMMA-PU- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticle mixture. The encapsulation layer was deposited onto PSC devices with a simple solution method. The ultrahydrophobic layer blocks the penetration of water molecules from the external environment. The role of the moderately hydrophilic layer in contact with the PSC is to capture the residual moisture within the perovskite layer. The complementary roles of these
two layers allow the PSCs to remain dry throughout operation. PSCs with the double layer exhibit a negligible change in photovoltaic (PV) performance after \(180\,\mathrm{min}\) while exposed to over \(95\%\) humidity. Under identical conditions, control samples without the double layer coating lose more than \(90\%\) of their power conversion capability after \(60\,\mathrm{min}\). In this work, the chemical and structural characteristics of each passivation layer are systematically examined and their roles on the overall water resistance are also discussed.
## 2 Results and discussion
The initial performance of bare PSCs and PMMA-coated and PMMA-PU-coated devices was tested under AM 1.5 conditions in \(35\%\) humidity. The short circuit current \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\), open circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\), fill factor (FF), and PCE values for the bare PSCs are \(22.3\,\mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\), \(1.09\,\mathrm{V}\), \(0.75\), and \(18.2\%\), respectively. The perovskite can be easily decomposed by polar solvents due to the lower solubility of the perovskite in hydrogen-bonded
solvents and reactions between polar aprotic solvents and \(\mathrm{Pb}_{2}^{+}\)cations [32]. Therefore, non-polar solvents such as toluene, chlorobenzene, and diethyl ether are better for coating the passivation layer on top of the perovskite phase. However, non-polar solvents have a problem of damaging the HTM \((2,2',7,7'\)-tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenyl-amine)-9,9'-spirobifluorene (spiroMeOTAD)) layer, which must be prevented. To suppress the dissolution of HTM during the coating process of the encapsulation layer, the solute concentration in the non-polar solvent was increased. Though a very small portion of HTM could be dissolved by the coating solution, change in the device performance on coating of the encapsulation layer was negligible. Figure S2 in the ESM shows \(J_{-}V\) curves of the PMMA/PSCs which used chlorobenzene base coating solutions with different PMMA content. As the content of the solute (PMMA) in chlorobenzene increases, the effect of the passivation coating on the initial performance of PSCs becomes negligible. A decrease in \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of PSCs treated with pure chlorobenzene indicates that the HTM layer (spiro-OMeTAD) can be dissolved by the pure non-polar solvent. However, when the PMMA concentration exceeds a critical point (35 wt.% PMMA), the passivation layer coating process has a negligible impact on the PCE of PSCs. Figure S3 in the ESM shows the cross-sectional SEM image of the PSCs before and after encapsulation with 35 wt.% PMMA concentration. After encapsulation, a 140 nm thick HTM layer was found on the top of the perovskite layer. A change in \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF before and after the encapsulation process is smaller than a standard deviation of \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF of pure PSCs. This indicates that the structural integrity of PSCs is maintained during the coating process of the encapsulation layer. A change in the PCE of pristine
PSCs, PMMA-, and PMMA-PU-coated PSCs in ambient conditions with over 95% humidity is shown in Fig. 1(c). The PCE of bare PSCs decreases from 18.2% to 1% within 60 min after exposure to over 95% humidity. In contrast, devices with PMMA and PMMA-PU coatings suppress degradation and the passivation effect of PMMA-PU coating is better than that of PMMA coating. After 180 min, the PCE for PMMA-PU devices exceeded 10%. PSC degradation in humid air is typically attributed to dissociation of the perovskite material itself. To examine the reaction between the halide perovskite and water, the optical properties and crystal structure of the perovskite layer are examined with and without the encapsulation layer.
Figures 2(a) and 2(b) show the absorption spectra for PSC devices with PMMA and PMMA-PU layers. The perovskite material under the PMMA layer quickly loses the ability to absorb visible light. In contrast, when the PMMA-PU layer is coated on the PSC, the absorption spectra decrease more slowly. This data suggests the PMMA-PU layer is more effective at suppressing water permeation and degradation of the perovskite phase compared with pure PMMA. Crystal structure analysis further confirms the conservation of the perovskite material through the encapsulation coating. Figure 2(c) shows X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of PMMA- and PMMA-PU-coated perovskite films. Peaks from the perovskite phase disappear in the PMMA-coated PSCs after the sample is exposed to over 95% humidity. However, the XRD pattern of the PMMA-PU-coated perovskite film is identical to the pristine perovskite film even after aging in over 95% humidity for 180 min.
PMMA is a structural polymer that is often used for device encapsulation. However, our results show
that the PMMA layer cannot effectively protect the perovskite material from water damage. The degradation shown in Figs. 1 and 2 suggests that the PMMA layer allows for water permeation under very humid conditions. Figure 3 shows images of the pure PMMA and PMMA-PU films obtained from three-dimensional (3D) optical profiling. The films were stored in over \(95\%\) humidity for 30 and \(60\;\mathrm{min}\). Control
samples aged without water are also displayed. Cracks are observed only in the PMMA film that is soaked in water for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). Damage of the PMMA film by water exposure occurs because a part of PMMA molecular chain is hydrophilic. Water molecules hydrolyze the ester groups within the PMMA structure, which in turn initiates swelling and breakdown of the PMMA film [33–36]. Cracks found in Figs. 3(b) and 3(c) indicate that continuous hydrolysis of these ester groups causes formation of extended defects and growth of macroscale cracks under very humid conditions (Fig. S4 in the ESM). Cracks within the PMMA provide further paths for water permeation into the film. A change in the surface roughness in Fig. S4 in the ESM also suggests that exposure to humid environments can change the microstructure of the PMMA coating. The PMMA-PU film does not exhibit large cracks (Figs. 3(e) and 3(f)), which indicates that the addition of PU slows down water permeation. This is due to the interpenetration of PU and PMMA molecule chains. Mixing of two different kinds of molecule chains form tangled and interconnected fibrillar networks [37, 38]. The stiffness of this interpenetrating polymer network is higher than the original individual polymers, and the free volume between polymer chains for water permeation is decreased [39–41]. Consequently, water diffusion from the ambient environment to the perovskite film through the interpenetrating network
is slowed down and the degradation of the perovskite phase under the PMMA-PU encapsulation layer is suppressed.
Although the PMMA-PU layer substantially prevents the degradation of PSCs, the PMMA-PU coated PSCs still exhibit \(30\%\) decrease in PCE after exposure to humid conditions. This result suggests that the permeation of water molecules proceeds in PMMA-PU at a significantly slower rate, but does not stop. The hydrophilic group within the PMMA-PU composite still attracts water molecules. As such, the PMMA-PU layer does not completely block the degradation of PSCs by water. This indicates that the moderate hydrophilicity of PMMA needs to be changed. In the present study, we convert PMMA to ultrahydrophobic PMMA by adding a small amount of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles. The effect of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles on the water wettability of PMMA films is presented in Fig. 4. The initial contact angle of the PMMA-PU film is \(68^{\circ}\) and is increased by the addition of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles. As the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticle content increases from \(15\ \mathrm{vol.}\%\) to \(20\ \mathrm{vol.}\%\), the contact angle dramatically increases and the mixture film turns to ultrahydrophobic. When the film is composed of \(50\ \mathrm{vol.}\%\) \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles, the contact angle is \(129^{\circ}\). This change in contact angle after addition of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles is due to reorientation of the polymer chains. When the high surface area nanoparticles are mixed with PMMA, the oxygen containing moieties such as the ester group rotate toward the oxide nanoparticle surface and form bond through van der Waals interactions [42]. As a result, the ester groups bonded to \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles can no longer attract water molecules and the hydrophilicity of PMMA is dramatically reduced. As such, PMMA hydrolysis ceases in humid environments.
The ultrahydrophobic PMMA-PU-\(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticle mixture film was then used as the encapsulation layer for PSCs. The approach was to endow ultrahydrophobicity only to the thin surface portion of the PMMA-based encapsulation layer. This ensures that water cannot permeate into the PMMA layer. At the same time, the bottom hydrophilic portion of the encapsulation layer can also act as a desiccant which removes residual water within PSCs that has accrued during synthesis and storage. As a result, the amphiphilic PMMA coating is expected to enhance the water resistance of the encapsulation coating without deteriorating the useful structural properties of PMMA. Figure 5(a) shows an SEM image for the cross-section of the double-layer coated PSCs. The bottom portion in contact with the perovskite layer is PMMA-PU. The top portion of the encapsulation layer in contact with the external environment is the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticle PMMA-PU composite. The double encapsulation film is prepared with a two-step coating process. The \(7\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) thick PMMA-PU layer was first spin-coated. Then the \(2.5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) thick PMMA-PU-\(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticle mixture layer was deposited. No damage to the underlying perovskite layer was observed under the PMMA-based encapsulation coating.
Figure 5(b) shows that when the amphiphilic double layer is used for the encapsulation of PSCs in over \(95\%\) humidity conditions, the PCE does not decrease after \(180\ \mathrm{min}\). This result indicates that the additional ultrahydrophobic portion of the encapsulation efficiently expels water from the PSC surface in humid environments. In addition, we have analyzed the microstructure of perovskite films with/without encapsulation in high-humidity environments. When the PSCs are exposed to over \(95\%\) humidity without an encapsulation layer, the film becomes porous, as shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, and is consistent with literature [23]. Under ambient humidity conditions the perovskite phase is transformed to \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and residual organic components. Some of the residual organic components are released in a gaseous state and thus structural voids are left in the film. These changes in the crystal structure and microstructure of the perovskite film increases series resistance and decreases shunt resistance, leading to a low PV performance. On the other hands, the
microstructure of the PSCs with the double layer is maintained and a noticeable degradation in PV performance is not observed. A schematic description of the proposed mechanism is presented in Fig. 5(c). For comparison, Table S2 in the ESM summarizes recent efforts to use encapsulation layers to enhance water resistance. One previous study showed an encapsulated device with a PCE of \(15.7\%\) that started to degrade within \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) after being submerged in water and after \(100\,\mathrm{h}\) in \(75\%\) humidity. In this study, however, PSCs with a double layer coating maintains a PCE of \(17.3\%\) after exposure to over \(95\%\) humidity for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) . This data demonstrates the amphiphilic double layer film to be a more effective encapsulation layer when compared with PMMA-PU films.
To monitor the interaction with water, FTIR spectroscopy was performed in an attenuated total
reflectance mode (ATR) for PMMA, PMMA-PU, and double layer films that were coated on perovskite layers. Figure 6 shows ATR-FTIR spectra as function of aging time in over \(95\%\) humidity. For PMMA, the intensity of a broad band between \(3,000{-}3,600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) continuously increases. This IR band represents the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching mode for water, which further confirms that water molecules are adsorbed inside the PMMA film. In PMMA-PU films, the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching mode is not noticeable until the film is exposed to water for \(60\;\mathrm{min}\) . However, for the double layer films, no change in the ATR-FTIR spectra is observed after water exposure. This is because the ultrahydrophobic layer prevents water molecules from accessing the inside of the double layer coating.
It is also found that the double layer endows a self-recovery function to PSCs. To demonstrate this
function, the perovskite layer was first intentionally degraded at over \(95\%\) humidity for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\). Then, we coated different encapsulation layers on the degraded perovskite film. Optical micrographs in the upper row of Fig. 7(a) show as-aged perovskite layers without encapsulation and with the encapsulation layer (PMMA, PMMA-PU, PMMA-PU- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)/PMMA-PU double layer, and PMMA-PU- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)). Before heating, the color of all aged perovskite films was greyish with small yellow spots. The aged films with and without the encapsulation layer were annealed at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) in air at \(30\%\) humidity. After heating, the color of the perovskite films changed. The films in contact with the PMMA, PMMA-PU, and double layers became dark due to the recovery of the perovskite phase which absorbs the entire range of the visible spectrum. A slight difference in the color among them is due to a small change in the refractive index of the encapsulation layer. In contrast, the color of samples without the coating layer or with only PMMA- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) did not change during thermal annealing, since the coating layer did not adsorb the residual water or there was a continuous influx of water from the humid environment. In addition, in only the PMMA+PU+ \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) layer, water penetration is not prevented very effectively and the self-recovery effect cannot be achieved. Figure S6 in the ESM shows the degradation behavior of pristine and encapsulated PSCs. Though the PMMA+PU+ \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) layer increases the stability of PSCs, it is not as effective as the dual layer coating. However, the optical micrographs in the lower row of the Fig. 7(a) indicate that samples covered with PMMA, PMMA-PU, and double layer coatings recovered their original perovskite color after annealing. This color recovery is attributed to the desiccating effect of the PMMA layer [43]. Since PMMA can adsorb water, the water in the aged perovskite film can move into the PMMA layer that is in contact with the perovskite film. To investigate the effect of an encapsulation layer with a hydrophilic portion, we have analyzed the crystal structure and PV performance of the perovskite films which were exposed to conditions of \(>95\%\) humidity for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) and subsequently annealed at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) in ambient air. Figure 7(b) shows the XRD patterns from the perovskite films; 1) as deposited, 2) after exposure to the humid ambient conditions, and 3) after exposure
to the humid ambient conditions and post thermal annealing. One set of perovskite exposures to the ambient humid conditions and thermal annealing experiments was done for the films in contact with the hydrophilic portion of the encapsulation layer. When the film was exposed to over \(95\%\) humidity for only \(5\,\mathrm{min}\), the ratio of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) (110)/PbI\(_2\) (001) significantly decreased due to the degradation of the perovskite layer. Interestingly, the ratio of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) (110)/PbI\(_2\) (001) recovered to \(98\%\) of the initial value when the perovskite film encapsulated with the PMMA-PU layer was annealed at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In addition, PSCs in contact with the hydrophilic PMMA-PU layer recovered \(82\%\) of its initial PCE after the thermal annealing, as shown in Fig. 7(d). This clearly indicates that PMMA-PU can help the self-recovery of perovskite films by removing intercalated residual water. As a
result, the crystal structure of the perovskite layer returns to its original state. This data suggest that the PMMA-PU- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\) PMMA-PU double layer coating protects the perovskite film from humidity and also aids the recovery of water-contaminated perovskite films when the PSCs operates under incident radiation from the sun.
In the multi-layer film in this study, the PMMA-PU works as a relatively hydrophilic layer and the PMMA+PU+SiO\(_2\) works as a hydrophobic layer. These characteristics of the dual coating impact PSCs in three ways. First, because of the more hydrophobic property of the top layer, the proposed dual coating effectively prevents water present in ambient conditions from penetrating the perovskite layer. Second, the underlying hydrophilic layer traps water that goes through the upper layer of the dual coating and delays further water penetration. Third, residual water in the perovskite layer can move toward the underlying hydrophilic layer during normal operation of solar cells, leading to the self-healing of the structural damage of the perovskite layer. Therefore, the advantages of the multi-layer coating are i) more effective prevention of water penetration of the perovskite layer when exposed to ambient conditions and ii) the self-recovery of the perovskite layer. These, in turn, improve the stability of the PSCs, most notably in humid and hot environments.
## 3 Conclusion
In summary, a double layer structure consisting of a moderately hydrophilic PMMA-PU layer and an ultrahydrophobic PMMA-PU- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) layer serves two specific functions as the encapsulation film of PSCs. First, the ultrahydrophobic portion in contact with environments serves to repel water in humid environments and prevent water permeation into the vulnerable perovskite layer. Second, the moderately hydrophilic PMMA layer can act as a desiccant to extract residual water from the perovskite layer itself during the solar cell operation. The measured optical, structural, and photoelectrochemical characterizations show that the encapsulation coating and the underlying perovskite layer are very stable in extremely humid environments. The optimal encapsulation material in
this study exhibited a \(17.3\%\) conversion efficiency after aging in over \(95\%\) humidity for more than \(180\;\mathrm{min}\).
## 4 Experimental section
## 4.1 Preparation of \(\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite solution
\(\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3})P b I_{3}}\) perovskite solution \((40\;\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) was prepared through the reaction between methylammonium iodide (MAI) and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) . Both reagents were dissolved at a molar ratio of 1:1 in a \(7{\cdot}3\;(\mathrm{v/v})\) mixture of N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO). The reaction solution was stirred for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) at room temperature.
## 4.2 Solar cell fabrication
FTO glass (Pilkington, TEC-8, \(8\,\mathrm{G}\cdot\mathrm{sq}^{-1}\)) was sequentially washed for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) each in acetone, deionized water, and ethyl alcohol using an ultrasonic bath. After washing, the FTO was treated with ultraviolet-ozone (UVO) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) . The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) compact layer \(\left(\mathrm{cp-TiO}_{2}\right)\) was prepared by spin coating \(0.3\,\mathrm{M}\) titanium disopropoxide dis(acetylacetonate) in 1-butanol on FTO at \(3{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{s}\) . The coated FTO was then dried at \(130\,^{\circ}\!C\) for 5 min and then annealed at \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) . The substrate was then treated with a \(20\;\mathrm{mM}\) aqueous \(\mathrm{TiCl_{4}}\) solution at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) . After treatment, the substrate was washed with deionized water and ethyl alcohol and then sintered at \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) . The mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer \(\left(\mathrm{mp-TiO}_{2}\right)\) was prepared by spin coating the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) paste solution (ShareChem, SC-HT040) that was dispersed in anhydrous ethyl alcohol \((1{:}5.5\;\mathrm{w/w})\) on top of the \(\mathrm{cp-TiO}_{2}\) layer at \(5{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(40\;\mathrm{s}\) . The samples were then annealed at \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) . Subsequently, a completely dissolved \(\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3})P b I_{3}}\) perovskite solution was spin-coated on the \(\mathrm{mp-TiO}_{2}\) layer at \(4{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(20\;\mathrm{s}\) . During the accelerated spinning step, the substrate was treated with \(0.3\;\mathrm{mL}\) of diethyl ether by drop-casting. The \(\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3})P b I_{3}}\) adduct film was baked at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\) and \(100\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) . The HTL solution formulation consisted of \(36\;\mathrm{mg}\) of spiroMeOTAD, \(14.4~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 4-tert-butylpyridine (tBP), and \(8.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonamide lithium salt (Li-TFSI) solution \((0.72\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) in acetonitrile). This mixture was dissolved in \(0.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) chlorobenzene
and spin coated \((25\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) on the \((\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3})\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) layer at \(4{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(40\,\mathrm{s}\). Finally, an \(80\,\mathrm{nm}\)-thick Au electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation at \(\sim10^{-6}\) bar through a shadow mask.
## 4.3 Encapsulation layer coating
PMMA (molecular weight \(=15{,}000\)) was slowly added to a stirred solution of chlorobenzene in an ultrasonic bath to make a completely dissolved \(35\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) solution. The photocurable polyurethane (NOA. 61, Norland) was added to the PMMA solution to strengthen the rigidity of the PMMA polymer chains by forming an interpenetrating network. The solution was stirred in the dark to prevent unnecessary solidification by light illumination. To prepare a PMMA-PU coating solution, NOA. 61 was dissolved in chlorobenzene first and PMMA were subsequently added and stirred in the dark. The encapsulation solution was spin-coated on \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) solar cells at \(3{,}000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\). The film was then cured for \(3\,\mathrm{min}\) under a UV lamp (X Lite™ 500). For the PMMA- \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) composite coating, \(50\,\mathrm{nm}\,\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) particles were first prepared using a previously reported method [44]. For the ultrahydrophobic layer coating, \(35\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) PMMA and PU powders were dissolved in \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) methyl isobutyl ketone (MIBK) and \(5\,\mathrm{mL}\) 2-propanol (IPA) and the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles were dispersed in the mixture solution. The volume ratio of PMMA and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) was 1:1.
## 4.4 Characterization
Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, JSM-7600F, JEOL) was used to analyze sample morphology and thickness. The surface roughness of the encapsulation layer was examined by atomic force microscopy (AFM, XE-7, Park system). The wetting properties of the encapsulation layer were evaluated with contact angle analysis (Contact angle, Kruss, DSA100). The absorbance spectrum of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) on glass was measured by ultraviolet visible (UV–Vis) spectroscopy (Perkin Elmer, Lamda 35). The relative humidity of the ambient conditions was maintained very high by keeping the PSCs in an enclosed container filled with water. PSCs were first placed on a small glass plate and the plate was floated in the enclosed large container. Then, the large container was heated on the hotplate at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) to reach \(100\%\) relative humidity, as shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM. During the long-term stability measurements, relative humidity inside the large container was monitored with a hygrometer and was measured at over \(95\%\). The \(J{-}V\) characteristics of the PSCs with a metal mask (active area of \(0.14\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) were measured under solar-simulated light (Newport Oriel Solar 3A Class AAA, 64023A) with the aid of a potentiostat (CHI 600D, CH Instruments). The AM 1.5G sun light \((100\,\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) was calibrated using a standard Si-solar cell (Oriel, VLSI standards). The crystal structure of \((\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3})\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) perovskite material was investigated by powder XRD (D-MAS 2500, Rigaku) with a \(\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha\) target. The angle ranged from \(10^{\circ}\) to \(40^{\circ}\) in \(2\theta\) range.
